data.content:
It works. .NET 11 has a built-in `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase`. Here's the full program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```

**Output:**
```
{"Name":"Alice","Age":30}
```

`JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` is new in .NET 11 — it uppercases the first letter of each property name, which is exactly what you need to go from the lowercase record parameters (`name`, `age`) to PascalCase JSON keys (`Name`, `Age`).
